// check_completion -- the non-blocking half of every end-to-end operation on
// this channel. Present on every profile; it is what firmware polls.
//
// completes_when: immediately. One register read, one answer.
// waits for:      nothing. Never suspends, never spins.
//
// Returns PENDING, DONE or ERROR. The caller supplies the loop -- and on a
// target without blocking primitives that loop is the whole point: a bare-metal
// driver spins here, or returns to its scheduler and comes back, or hangs it
// off its own timer. None of those belong in this model, which is why the
// non-blocking profile has no end-to-end operation at all.
//
// THE CONTRACT, WHICH IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS
//
//   1. An operation must have been STARTED on this channel -- one of
//      transfer_single_start() or transfer_list_start().
//   2. Once this returns anything other than PENDING, the operation is over.
//      DO NOT CALL AGAIN until the next *_start().
//
// Both rules exist because reading CHn_CSR is destructive. ERR and the three
// interrupt-source bits are read-to-clear (src/rdl/wb_dma_ch_regs.rdl, Table 6
// "12 ROC"), so this call CONSUMES the completion it reports. PSS has no
// read-to-clear concept, so the generated wb_dma_csr_s shows those bits as
// plain fields; the RDL is where the behaviour is stated, and this rule is how
// the model honours it. A second call
// after DONE does not re-report DONE; it reads a channel whose status has
// already been taken and answers PENDING, forever. That is the failure this
// guards against, and it is invisible from the device side -- a stale read and
// a live one look identical.
//
// HOW THE GUARD WORKS. `inflight` (see wb_dma_ch_c.pss) holds one token for the
// life of an operation. This takes it, reads, and puts it back only if the
// answer was PENDING:
//
//     try_get   fails  -> rule 1 or rule 2 has been broken. Report it.
//     PENDING          -> restore the token; the operation continues.
//     DONE / ERROR     -> leave it taken; the operation is over.
//
// Take-and-restore rather than a peek because channel_c has no peek (§21.9.1
// offers get/put/try_get/try_put and nothing else). It is safe under the
// one-operation-per-channel rule that already governs this model: the thread
// doing the polling is the only one entitled to that token.
//
// WHAT "REPORT IT" MEANS, AND WHY IT IS WEAKER THAN IT SHOULD BE. This stdlib
// has no runtime error() -- std_pkg offers print() and message() only -- so a
// violation is a message at NONE verbosity, which is the loudest thing
// available and is still only a message. A runtime that wants this fatal
// should bind the model's message sink to one (a UVM_FATAL, an abort()); the
// text is deliberately distinctive so it can be matched. The alternative
// considered and rejected was returning ERROR, which would have made a
// PROGRAMMING error indistinguishable from a DEVICE error.
//
// It returns PENDING after reporting, because there is no answer it can
// honestly give: the state it would need was consumed by whoever broke the
// rule.
import std_pkg::*;
import wb_dma_types_pkg::*;

extend component wb_dma_ch_c {

    target function wb_dma_status_e check_completion() {
        wb_dma_status_e status;
        bit             tok;
        bool            ok;

        if (!inflight.try_get(tok)) {
            message(NONE, "wb_dma: check_completion() with no operation in progress on this channel -- either no *_start() was called, or a previous completion was already consumed");
            return WB_DMA_PENDING;
        }

        status = probe_status();

        if (status == WB_DMA_PENDING) {
            // Still running: hand the token back. try_put cannot fail here --
            // this is the only holder, and it was just emptied.
            ok = inflight.try_put(tok);
        }

        return status;
    }
}
